Participant: Kedar Shahi Â· Exported: 11/2/2026, 11:05:54 AM

## Nadia Brennan Â· Senior Premium Auditor

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:55 AM

Hello, how are you today?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:55 AM

I run premium audits on new business, so I can walk you through how we check an employer's payroll. I have another employer call after this so I have about thirty minutes today, and until Friday's quotes go out, I am only free for this work before ten in the morning or after three in the afternoon.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:55 AM

Nadia, can you explain where I can find the six employer files and your company culture and roles?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:55 AM

I am a Senior Premium Auditor at Linden Shore Mutual, where I classify workers and audit payroll. The employer files are located in the employer files link.

- [employer files](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/REDACTED)

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:56 AM

How do you decide which class code an employer belongs to?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:56 AM

I classify people by what they actually do, not by their job titles. Employers usually report their payroll split leaning toward the clerical code because it is the cheapest, but I check the actual duties performed.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:56 AM

How do you calculate and audit payrolls including overtime, bonus, and subcontractors?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:56 AM

Payments to a subcontractor who cannot show a certificate of insurance covering the whole audit period are added to the employer's payroll at sixty percent of the amount paid, under the code for the work the sub did.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

Where can I find the Linden Shore Mutual office class code rules and pedagogue identifiers?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

The classification guide excerpt, which contains the code list and the wording of each code, is available in the classification guide excerpt link.

- [classification guide excerpt](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

How do you handle overtime bonus investment employers who perform different types of work?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

At a contractor doing both roofing and siding, a worker with fifteen or more roof days goes under R-three ten, and one with fewer goes under R-three twenty. We count roof days from timesheets and never split pay between the codes.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

If an employer payroll split is incorrect, whose records should I use to correct it?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:57 AM

When an employer's records and its application disagree, the employer's records decide the allocation.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

Are there any specific classification rules for construction workers, electricians, custodians, restaurant employers, and legal office staff?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

The caterer code F-720 applies only to an employer whose off-site catering is forty percent or more of gross receipts. For office staff, they keep the clerical code C-one ten only if they are on a job site on four days or fewer.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

What should I do when the records in completed two records contradict each other?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

The records in the file decide the allocation, and the contradiction goes on the employer's question list for them to confirm.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

What information must appear in your final audit report and which adjustments require employer confirmation?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:58 AM

I do the classification and payroll split. Every adjustment I make, and every record a decision rests on, goes on the question list for the employer to confirm. Underwriting sends these six quotes out on Friday. Before then I have time to call only two of the employers, so I need to know which two to call first and why. Which ones is your call; I'll want your reasons.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

Whose records should I review to identify the two employers with highest audit risk?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

Review the employer's payroll register, business description, field notes, and any logs or timesheets to find where the application and records disagree.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

Where can I access the incomplete internal class version rules including all class code and paragraph reference?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

As I mentioned earlier, the classification guide excerpt with the class code list and wording is available in the classification guide excerpt link.

- [classification guide excerpt](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

How should I payroll the employers when there are multiple payroll discrepancies or missing insurance certificates?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 10:59 AM

Missing certificates mean you add sixty percent of the amount paid to the employer's payroll under the code for that work.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:00 AM

Can you clarify the audit rule for clerical employees who visit job sites, including how days are counted?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:00 AM

An office employee keeps the clerical code C-one ten only if they are on a job site on four days or fewer. On five or more days, their whole payroll moves to the employer's governing field code.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:00 AM

How should I handle overtime, premium, bonus, reimbursement when they're calculating payroll?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:00 AM

A daily allowance paid to crews working out of town is left out of payroll up to forty-seven dollars per person per day, and anything above that amount counts as payroll.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

If an employer performed different types of work, what records are required to split the payroll?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

You need the payroll register, business description, field notes, and logs or timesheets for the actual duties to split the payroll. Underwriting sends these six quotes out on Friday. Before then I have time to call only two of the employers, so I need to know which two to call first and why. Which ones is your call; I'll want your reasons.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

How do you determine the governing field code when an employer performs multiple types of work?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

The governing field code is the one that carries the most payroll at that specific employer.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

If the supporting records are incomplete, should I leave the allocation on slope or make provisional allocation?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

Propose how you would treat it, write down why, and mark it as an open item for me or the employer to confirm.

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

Is there anything else I need to know to accurately classify all six employers and prepare the audit reports?

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:01 AM

I do not know anything beyond the provided records and rules, so propose open items as they arise. Are you done, or do you have last questions before I head off?

**Kedar Shahi** Â· Voice Â· 11:02 AM

Okay, I'll review the records, prepare the six audits, and identify the two employers who need calls first.

**Nadia Brennan** Â· Voice Â· 11:02 AM

Good luck with your audits.
